     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin blocks exfiltration. The auth_db, auth_fileserver and auth_backup techniques each require (NOT mfa_dbadmin OR admin_dc OR admin_adfs). With mfa_dbadmin deployed, the attacker would need admin_dc or admin_adfs. Those need foothold_dc or foothold_adfs, which come from auth_dc and auth_adfs, and both require NOT mfa_admin. So with both MFA controls deployed, no exfiltration server can be reached. No single control is enough. Segmented alone fails because phishing gives a workstation foothold, unpatched escalation gives admin_workstation, and that satisfies pivot_internal. mfa:user alone fails because a phished workstation foothold bypasses it. Then kerberoasting gives stolen_admin, and escalate_ciserver plus leak_secrets give stolen_dbadmin. mfa:admin alone fails because stolen_dbadmin from the CI server still allows auth_db, since mfa_dbadmin is not deployed. mfa:dbadmin alone fails because auth_dc, escalate_dc and dump_dbadmin_dc give admin_dc, which bypasses it. No single patch is enough either. The db, fileserver and backup escalations never check patches, and the attacker always has an alternative entry point or escalation path. So the minimum is 2.\"}"
    }
   ],
